WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.734
-Thank you for coming back
to the show.

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:04.434
-Thank you for having me back.
-What are you talking about?

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:09.400
Since we've last talked on the
show, you've had a crazy ride.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.300
-Yeah.
-Yeah. Congratulations.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.601
You won an Oscar.
-Thank you.

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:15.267
I did. I did. I did. I did.

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:16.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:17.501
Yeah!

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:18.701
-Look at this.
-I know. Crazy.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:19.934
So did that guy over there.

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:21.634
He won an Oscar, too.
-Questlove got one, too.

00:00:21.634 --> 00:00:23.567
-No, no, no.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.067
-Come on! That's amazing!

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:27.434
-That was a great night.
-Yeah.

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:30.901
-You made history as being
the first Afro-Latina

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.801
and first
openly queer woman of color

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:34.367
to win an Oscar for acting.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.934
I know it's a mouthful,
but thank you.

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:39.133
I appreciate it.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.334
-No, you know, but I wanted to
tell you out here

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:44.300
that your speech was so moving

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.801
and so perfectly said
and well-put.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:52.567
Everyone was crying happy tears
and just so psyched and proud.

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:53.834
I mean, what did that feel

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:55.868
and how did you feel
when you got off the stage?

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:57.534
You're like -- Did you know
that you nailed it?

00:00:57.534 --> 00:00:58.834
Because you nailed it.

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:00.734
-You know,
it was really surreal.

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:02.267
-Yeah, it was.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.901
-God, I don't even
really know how to describe it.

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:07.734
-Do you remember them
saying your name

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.167
and you getting up and --

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.100
-I did.
I really just wanted to cry.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.300
But that's not
the moment you cry.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.534
You get up.

00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:15.634
You've got to walk
and you've got to say something.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:19.400
And I remember thinking,
"Oh, this is the time

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.634
to say what you want to say."

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:25.734
And that's what I wanted to say,
that there was a place for us.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:28.701
And it's such a,
like, point of joy for me,

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:29.968
especially right now,

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:32.167
because we've got to
hold on to our joy, people.

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah, we do.

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.234
You got to hold on to your joy.
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:38.467
-So, I was just so grateful.

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:40.234
And, I mean,
I never expected to be

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:42.200
standing in the same spot
Julia Roberts stood.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.267
You know what I mean?

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:46.000
Like, Denzel Washington
stood there.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Like, it was just --
I don't know.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.234
It was really cool.

00:01:48.234 --> 00:01:50.367
-But, also, I loved the detail
of your speech.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.901
It was like, "To the little girl
in the back of a --"

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:54.000
Was it a Ford --

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.501
-A Ford Focus,
a white Ford Focus.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:56.634
-A white Ford Focus.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:58.601
That immediately set
the stage for me.

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.300
And I go, "Oh, I love you."

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.200
I love that you said that,
'cause that is exactly real.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.834
That's descriptive, and it was
like, it really happened to you.

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:06.133
-Well, it's good to remind
people, like,

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.634
who you are, you know?

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:09.300
It's like, yes, I get dressed
and I look like this every day,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.033
but, like, that's a real person.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.100
That's a real little girl.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.868
That little girl
still exists, you know?

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:15.234
Like, and there's a little girl

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.033
out there in
a white Ford Focus right now,

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:18.934
and she needs to know that
her dreams are possible.

00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:19.934
-That's exactly right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:19.934 --> 00:02:21.067
-Yeah.

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.567
-That's what I felt.
That's what came across.

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:24.200
I loved it.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.901
So, now you have the Oscar.

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:28.601
Where do you keep the Oscar?

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:30.400
Is this one of those things
where, like,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.033
it's a doorstop or you --

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:33.834
-Oh, no.
-Toilet paper on it.

00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:35.834
-I am so not there yet.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.701
Like, I know that works for
Dame Emma Thompson,

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.100
who I happen to love.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.133
Like, work Queen.

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.234
But, for me, I'm like,
"Nah, this is too important."

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:48.367
So, I actually have
a fully functioning fireplace,

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:49.534
believe it or not,

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:51.067
that's either
really dangerous or awesome.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:52.267
-In New York City?
-In New York City.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.667
-Good for you.
-Thank you so much. I love it.

00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:55.968
-Wow.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.734
-But I have it on my mantel,
in addition to the other,

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:01.901
like, blessings, the accolades
that I received this season.

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:06.501
And on my way to my coffeemaker
in the morning, I just pass by

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:09.300
and I'm like,
"Yeah, good things can happen."

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.200
Like, you work hard,
don't be a dick, be kind,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.868
you know, like, good things
can happen for people.

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:16.901
Put in the effort.

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:18.934
-Yeah, be kind,
be a good person.

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.868
-Yeah, just don't suck,
man, please.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:27.267
I am so tired of people sucking.

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:28.567
-Yes.
-Like, be nice.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:31.267
-Just be nice.
-Kindness is free, people.

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:33.100
It don't cost you nothing
to be kind.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.334
-That's exactly right.

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:37.601
-I can't do it. So...

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:41.567
-So, not only that, then
I saw you -- We were at a thing,

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:43.667
and I saw you and I go,
"Are you nervous for the Tonys?"

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.901
And you're like, "Yeah, I am.
I'm a little nervous."

00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:48.200
And I go,
"That's my favorite award show.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.534
It's live and it's --"
-It's great.

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:50.868
-Oh, I love it.
I love the energy.

00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:53.334
I love the whole -- I mean,
what talent in that room.

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:55.334
And to just, like, show off

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.100
in front of so many talented
people watching you,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.267
and just like --

00:03:58.267 --> 00:03:59.300
-Oh, I was just nervous.
I wanted to throw up.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.100
-You nailed it, buddy.
-Thank you.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.868
-You nailed it,
and I go, "Ooh, yes!"

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:05.667
And then you stuck the landing.
I go, "Oh, my gosh!"

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:06.801
It was so good.
-Oh, I love that.

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:08.734
For you,
it felt like a football game.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:10.701
-Yes.
-Like, "Touchdown!"

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:12.868
-Yeah, no, I was screaming.
I was like, "Ooh, yes!

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.033
Oh, nailed it.
Oh, that's funny.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.934
Oh, that's good.
Oh, you look good."

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:17.868
-Well, that means a lot to me,
because I'm not a comedian.

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:19.267
Like, I don't do what
you do every day.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:21.968
I'm not Whoopi Goldberg or
Billy Crystal for that matter.

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:24.434
So when they asked me to do it,
I was like,

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.067
"Oh, this is something
I didn't know I wanted to do."

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:29.167
-Yeah.
-But I like hosting.

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:30.467
And I like doing it my way,

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:33.334
'cause, not for nothing,
I like people.

00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:37.300
So, I just was like,
"Okay, let me do what I do."

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.834
And I know Broadway.

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:40.667
Like, I spent
10 years on Broadway.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.667
And I wanted to
create an evening

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:45.334
that was just celebratory
and brought some joy.

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:47.601
I know it's annoying,
but, like, joy.

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:49.200
-Yeah, it was
the first show back.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.534
-Back at Radio City.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:52.801
And I wanted it to be grand
and sexy and fun

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:54.467
and new and a little different.

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:57.667
And, not for nothing,
those folks working on Broadway

00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:00.701
eight times a week --
they deserved a good night.

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.934
-Exactly right.
They put the work in.

00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:05.634
-Yes.
So, I'm so glad you liked it.

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:06.734
-I loved it.

00:05:06.734 --> 00:05:09.367
You know,
there was a great quote I read

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:11.200
maybe from NPR or
something I heard from NPR,

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.534
and they said, "Ariana DeBose
should host everything."

00:05:14.534 --> 00:05:16.000
I was like, "That's fantastic."

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.133
-That's nervous.
That makes me nervous.

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:18.868
-But that's a good review
right there, yeah.

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:20.801
-I'll take it, but yikes!

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:22.634
-Yeah.
-Ew!

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:25.033
-It is --
Today is the last official day

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:26.567
of Pride Month,
and I know that you are

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:28.234
a big supporter and advocate.

00:05:28.234 --> 00:05:29.501
What comes to your mind

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:31.534
as we're getting to
the end of Pride Month?

00:05:31.534 --> 00:05:34.767
-You know, I'm thinking about
a lot at the end of Pride Month.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:39.400
And I think -- I just want to
remind, you know,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.901
all my LGBTQAI+
brothers and sisters, like,

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:44.767
don't stop being you.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.968
You know, fight for your dreams,
fight for your joy,

00:05:47.968 --> 00:05:50.968
and remember, like,
you are -- you're valid.

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:53.534
I say it a lot,
but it's important.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:59.934
And it's also important to
remember that we're in a fight.

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:04.300
You know, we can talk a lot
about how the Supreme Court

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.167
has just waged war on
women's reproductive rights,

00:06:07.167 --> 00:06:08.501
but the reality is,

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:12.033
Clarence Thomas was
very clear on what his plan is.

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:14.200
He's not just coming for women.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.667
They already came for
voting rights.

00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:17.000
They're going to continue.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.734
But they're coming for
marriage equality.

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:21.801
And, quite frankly,
they'll probably come for

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.834
adoption rights
by same-sex couples.

00:06:23.834 --> 00:06:26.033
So this is
the time to get in the fight,

00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:28.167
and it starts on a local level.

00:06:28.167 --> 00:06:30.100
So find ways to get involved.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.534
I personally,
because I -- Come on.

00:06:32.534 --> 00:06:37.000
I'm black. I'm Afro-Latina.
I'm a woman, and I'm queer.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.167
My country is at war with me,
and that's hard.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.567
Oh, my God, that's hard.

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:44.968
But the only thing I can do is
hold on to my joy

00:06:44.968 --> 00:06:47.000
and make a difference
at a local level.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.734
So I'm trying to find
the abortion funds

00:06:49.734 --> 00:06:52.000
in my community
that I can support

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.334
so I can help anyone
with a uterus,

00:06:54.334 --> 00:06:57.133
whether that means getting them
to a safe-haven state

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:59.133
or, in the states that don't
want to support them,

00:06:59.133 --> 00:07:00.534
how can we support them?

00:07:00.534 --> 00:07:05.167
So, I want to encourage folks to
research, get involved,

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:09.200
but do your part, because this
is not about one set of rights.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.567
It's about human rights.
-Yeah.

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:14.734
-And I will -- I'm sorry to be
on a soapbox,

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.467
but I'm not sorry.

00:07:16.467 --> 00:07:18.334
I'm fired up and I want
everybody else to be, too.

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:20.767
-Be fired up. Be fired up!

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:22.701
-I'm so fired up!
-Yeah, be fired up!

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:23.868
-But you know what?

00:07:23.868 --> 00:07:26.234
This is a terrible analogy,
but I like it.

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:28.634
This is a time of
planting seeds, right?

00:07:28.634 --> 00:07:30.701
Because seeds beget roots

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:32.834
and hopefully
really healthy trees.

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:38.367
We've got a rotten-ass tree
called the Supreme Court

00:07:38.367 --> 00:07:39.467
and we've got to root it out.

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:41.701
-Yeah.
-So do your part, people.

00:07:41.701 --> 00:07:43.434
-Yeah. That's right.

00:07:43.434 --> 00:07:45.834
Thank you. Thank you, though.
Thank you.

00:07:45.834 --> 00:07:46.901
Thank you. I appreciate this.

00:07:46.901 --> 00:07:49.033
I do also want to --

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:50.801
I want to talk about
"Westworld," by the way.

00:07:50.801 --> 00:07:52.434
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:52.434 --> 00:07:54.367
-You know we had
Evan Rachel Wood on the show.

00:07:54.367 --> 00:07:57.367
-I know, and she did this crazy
Janis Joplin impression.

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:58.534
-Was that amazing?
-Like, come on.

00:07:58.534 --> 00:08:00.133
She is incredible.
-Yeah, she was amazing.

00:08:00.133 --> 00:08:01.901
But she was like,
"Yeah, we have a great season."

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:04.934
And I did not know that you were
joining the "Westworld" gang.

00:08:04.934 --> 00:08:08.334
-Yeah, it was, like,
a surprise to me, too.

00:08:08.334 --> 00:08:10.334
No, for real.

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:12.000
We shot this
during the pandemic.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.267
And I was really waiting for
"West Side Story"

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:17.200
to be released to the world,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.267
because nobody really knew
what I did in the film.

00:08:20.267 --> 00:08:23.133
So, I got this
e-mail about making

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:25.901
a tape for an untitled project.

00:08:25.901 --> 00:08:27.734
And so I made
a one-minute video.

00:08:27.734 --> 00:08:30.033
And they -- It turned out
to be for "Westworld."

00:08:30.033 --> 00:08:31.033
-Yeah.

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:32.767
-And they were like, "Cool.

00:08:32.767 --> 00:08:34.968
So, we want you
to come be on the show

00:08:34.968 --> 00:08:38.100
for a character we
can't really tell you about."

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.834
And I was like, "Okay."

00:08:41.834 --> 00:08:42.868
-"Yes, I'm going to do this,"

00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:44.067
but you really have to
trust them.

00:08:44.067 --> 00:08:46.300
-Yeah. So, I chatted with
the showrunners

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:47.868
and I watched
the first season of the show.

00:08:47.868 --> 00:08:49.267
And I'm not going to lie.

00:08:49.267 --> 00:08:51.834
I got busy with a little show
called "Hamilton," so I --

00:08:51.834 --> 00:08:53.234
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:58.167
So, I didn't get to see
the subsequent seasons.

00:08:58.167 --> 00:09:00.934
But I liked what I saw and
I thought it was really special.

00:09:00.934 --> 00:09:02.100
-It's all so beautiful.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.467
They're almost like
they make movies, almost.

00:09:03.467 --> 00:09:04.501
They look like movies.
-It is.

00:09:04.501 --> 00:09:06.734
It's cinematic art,
in my opinion.

00:09:06.734 --> 00:09:08.767
And also what I learned
is that they shoot on film,

00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:10.267
and there aren't
many television shows

00:09:10.267 --> 00:09:12.868
who do shoot on film these days.

00:09:12.868 --> 00:09:14.801
-Yeah. "The Tonight Show" does.

00:09:14.801 --> 00:09:18.601
Yeah, our show and "Westworld."
We do all of our shows on film.

00:09:18.601 --> 00:09:21.200
Yeah. They want to really make
sure -- Yeah, we do film.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.000
But that's tricky.
-Yeah. It's tricky.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.767
And that just means you have to
show up and be present.

00:09:24.767 --> 00:09:28.801
We shot "West Side Story"
on film, so every take counts.

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:31.901
And I was like, "Okay,
I'm going to say yes to this."

00:09:31.901 --> 00:09:33.534
And I went on the ride.

00:09:33.534 --> 00:09:34.701
And I'm still on the ride

00:09:34.701 --> 00:09:37.734
because I have no idea
how the show turned out.

00:09:37.734 --> 00:09:40.501
So I'm very invested
in the season.

00:09:40.501 --> 00:09:42.133
-Yeah. I want to show
everyone a clip.

00:09:42.133 --> 00:09:44.133
Here's Ariana DeBose
and Evan Rachel Wood

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:45.868
in this Sunday's episode
of "Westworld."

00:09:45.868 --> 00:09:46.968
Take a look.

00:09:46.968 --> 00:09:48.234
-Couldn't sleep.

00:09:48.234 --> 00:09:50.167
I just kept
thinking about that guy.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.534
They told me I ruined his life.

00:09:54.534 --> 00:09:58.033
-Christina, if he confused
a game with his life,

00:09:58.033 --> 00:10:00.701
it's because his life
was already ruined.

00:10:00.701 --> 00:10:02.033
I mean, come on.

00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:04.467
Died suddenly, ex-wife.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.467
Hope Center for Mental Health
is his charity of choice.

00:10:08.467 --> 00:10:09.634
-How do you know that?

00:10:09.634 --> 00:10:11.400
-It's right here.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.300
In his obituary.

00:10:17.834 --> 00:10:21.767
-His story --
I think I might have

00:10:21.767 --> 00:10:23.133
written something like this.

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:25.133
-Doesn't change the fact
that he was certifiable.

00:10:25.133 --> 00:10:27.033
And you want to know how I know?

00:10:27.901 --> 00:10:29.868
He jumped off a roof.

00:10:31.634 --> 00:10:34.934
-Ooh!
Ariana DeBose, everybody.

00:10:34.934 --> 00:10:38.467
You can see "Westworld"
Sundays at 9:00 p.m. on HBO.

